window.BIZ_PAPERS = (window.BIZ_PAPERS || []).concat([{"id": "COMP2-SAM", "unit": 2, "key": "SAM", "series": "Sample assessment (AAQ)", "date": "Pearson sample assessment material", "title": "Pearson sample paper", "source": "pearson", "kind": "exam", "code": "Sample", "spec": "new", "total": 90, "minutes": 135, "items": [{"q": 1, "sub": "a(i)", "marks": 4, "section": "Q1", "ctx": ["One of Melanie’s responsibilities is to ensure that Redpool College’s data is stored securely. She uses encryption to help with this. Melanie could use symmetric or asymmetric encryption.", "Symmetric encryption uses a single key for both encryption and decryption.", "Asymmetric encryption uses two keys, one to encrypt and the other to decrypt."], "text": "Describe two other differences between symmetric and asymmetric\nencryption.", "ms": {"body": "For Difference 1 and Difference 2:\nAward one mark for each characteristic and one\nmark for an appropriate linked description of the\ndifference up to a maximum of two marks.\n• Cipher text (1) is larger in asymmetric\n(than symmetric) (1)\n• Encryption (process) (1) is faster for\nsymmetric (than asymmetric) (1)\n• Key length/size (1) is smaller in symmetric\n(than asymmetric)/is 128 or 256 for\nsymmetric and 2048 for asymmetric (1)\n• Resources used/needed (1) are lower for\nsymmetric (than asymmetric) (1)\n• efficiency (1) is greater for symmetric\n(than asymmetric) (1)\n• security (1) is greater for symmetric (than\nasymmetric) (1)\n• authenticity/non-repudiation (1) is greater\nfor asymmetric/does not exist in\nsymmetric (1)\nDo not accept answers about number/type of\nkeys needed.\nAccept reverse arguments, such as Cipher text is\nsmaller for symmetric (than asymmetric).\nDo not award both versions of an\nanswer.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "a(ii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q1", "ctx": ["Difference 1 Difference 2"], "text": "Melanie decides to use an Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) algorithm to\nprotect the data.\nAES can have key lengths of 256, 192 or 128 bits. A 256 bit key is more secure\nthan a 192 bit or 128 bit key.\nDescribe one situation where a 128 bit or 192 bit key would need to be used.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of two\nmarks.\n• Connection/internet/network speed/data\ntransmission rate is low/limited (1) and\nneeds a reduced data load (1)\n• Mobile/battery powered device is being\nused (1) and has less power/processing (1)\nAccept any other appropriate responses.\nDo not accept answers about security levels.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "a(iii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q1", "ctx": [], "text": "Using AES-256 implies having a 32 byte password. Most passwords are not\n32 bytes long.\nExplain how a 256 bit key is produced from a shorter password.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for identification of a process\nand one mark for an appropriate linked\nexpansion, up to a maximum of two marks.\nUse of a hashing algorithm/function (1) to expand\na short key (1)\nExpand a short key (1) by repeating the\nalgorithm/function until key length is long\nenough/256 bits (1)\nRepeat a hashing algorithm/function (1) until the\nkey length is long enough/256 bits (1)\nAccept any other appropriate responses.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "b(i)", "marks": 1, "section": "Q1", "ctx": ["Redpool College has 600 students who take classes at the campus and another", "1500 who are enrolled for distance learning.", "Melanie must ensure communications security for the 1500 distance learning", "students.", "One aspect of student communications is email. This uses Simple Mail Transfer", "Protocol (SMTP)."], "text": "Give one other protocol that is used by email.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for any of the following:\n• Internet Message Access (Protocol) (1)\n• Post Office (Protocol) (1)\n• Post Office Protocol 3 (1)\nAccept abbreviations: IMAP, POP, POP3.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "b(ii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q1", "ctx": [], "text": "Describe the role of SMTP in transmitting an email.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of two\nmarks.\n• Relays/accepts/routes message from\noriginator/user/sender (1)\n• Handshakes with/connects to\nreceiver/recipient (1)\n• Releases/delivers email to receiver (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "b(iii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q1", "ctx": [], "text": "SMTP is said to be inherently insecure.\nExplain the effect that this has on the college’s email.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for identification of a reason and\none mark for an appropriate linked expansion, up\nto a maximum of two marks.\n• Because the protocol has no security\nfeatures (1) it makes the email vulnerable\nto attack/interception (1)\n• Because of the lack of security (1)\n(sensitive) emails would need to be\nencrypted (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 1, "sub": "c", "marks": 9, "section": "Q1", "ctx": [], "text": "Melanie must ensure that Redpool College’s data is stored securely. The data is\nkept in a separate file server and is encrypted using the AES-256 algorithm.\nThe file server is also protected by a hardware firewall between it and the rest of\nthe network.\nEvaluate the effectiveness of this strategy in storing data securely and how it\nmight be improved.\nYour evaluation should include the benefits and drawbacks of the strategy, and a\nconclusion about how Redpool College could improve it.", "ms": {"body": "Award up to 9 marks.\nResponses should be in the context of securing Redpool College’s\ndata on a file server.\nResponses\nwill\nbe\ncredited\naccording\nto\nthe\nstudent’s\ndemonstration of knowledge and understanding of the material,\nusing the indicative content and level descriptors below.\nThe indicative content that follows is not prescriptive. Responses\nmay cover some or all indicative content, but students should be\nrewarded for other relevant responses.\nEncryption:\n• Encryption of data at rest, does not say what happens\nduring data movement\n• Does not say if individual file or whole disc encryption is\nused\n• Encryption ensures that even if data is looked at by\nunauthorised person, it remains unreadable without the\nappropriate decryption key.\n• It protects the confidentiality and integrity of the data,\nminimising the impact of breaches.\n• Weak point is password/authentication method, this is not\ngiven.\nFirewall:\n• First line of defence by monitoring incoming and outgoing\nnetwork traffic\n• Blocking or allowing data packets based on predefined\nsecurity rules.\n• Help to protect the network from unauthorised access and\n23 6\nmalicious traffic.\n• Hardware firewall usually more effective than software\n• Weak point is configuring the rules\n• Network should also have a firewall on its internet\nconnection\n• Network could have other hardware/software firewalls\noperating\n• Having two/multiple firewalls on a network should increase\nsecurity but could cause problems/clashes\n• Maintenance/update/patching issues\nConclusion/improvements (this is not a list of marking points\n- anything sensible and relevant should be accepted):\n• Ensure AES_256 or equivalent on moving data\n• Specify type of encryption, link to authorisation levels\n• Impose strong password policy/MFA\n• Check process/testing of firewall rules\n• Consider how firewalls are deployed to reduce/remove\nclashes\n• Ensure all firewalls are maintained/updated/patched to\nsame level\nMark scheme (award up to 9 marks) refer to guidance for how to apply levels-\nbased mark schemes*.\nLevel\nDescriptor\nLevel 0\nNo rewardable material.\nLevel 1\n1–3\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is partially relevant to the context of the\nquestion (AO2)\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis by superficially breaking\ndown the different aspects into component parts with\nerrors/inconsistency (AO3)\n• Demonstrates a basic evaluation which partially considers\ndifferent factors and competing points, leading to an\nunsupported conclusion (AO4).\nLevel 2\n4-6\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is relevant to the context of the\nquestion (AO2)\n• Demonstrates a good analysis by breaking down the\ndifferent aspects into component parts (AO3)\n• Demonstrates a good evaluation which considers different\nfactors and competing points, leading to a partially\n24\nsupported conclusion (AO4).\nLevel 3\n7-9\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of knowledge\nand understanding that is consistently relevant to the\ncontext of the question (AO2)\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis by comprehensively\nbreaking down the different aspects into their component\nparts (AO3)\n• Demonstrates a thorough evaluation which\ncomprehensively considers different factors and competing\npoints, leading to a well-supported conclusion (AO4).", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "a(i)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q2", "ctx": ["Redpool College has 600 students who take classes at the Redpool College campus and another 1500 who are enrolled for distance learning. Each student is given 25 Gb of personal storage when they join Redpool College. Each student is also given access to a storage area to use when working on assignments. The size of these areas can vary from a minimum of 1 Gb, up to 100 Gb, depending on the type of assignment. Melanie, the System Administrator, must ensure that there is enough data storage available and that the data is kept safe. The students’ personal storage area is kept on a data server at Redpool College."], "text": "Explain one security-related advantage for Redpool College in doing this.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for correct identification of the\nfirst point and one mark for an appropriate\nlinked justification, up to a maximum of two\nmarks.\n• Server is under (direct) control of Redpool\nCollege (1)\n• So easier to\n◦\nenforce password\nstrength/authorisation/MFA (1)\n◦\nprotect from physical\ntheft/unauthorised physical\nconnection (1)\n◦\nprotect from unauthorised\naccess/hacking (1)\nDo not accept answers that are not related to\nsecurity.\u0003", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "a(ii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q2", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain one security-related disadvantage for Redpool College in doing this.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for identification of a security-\nrelated disadvantage and one mark for an\nappropriate linked justification, up to a\nmaximum of two marks.\n• Could be a known/attractive target for\nattacking (1) as large amount of\ndata/number of student accounts (1)\n• Mistakes could expose (large amounts of)\ndata/accounts (1) as large number of\naccounts/passwords/authorisations to\nprocess/deal with (1)\nAccept any other appropriate responses.\nDo not accept answers that are not related to\nsecurity.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "a(iii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q2", "ctx": [], "text": "Give two ways by which the personal storage can be protected from loss due\nto failure of the data server.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each way up to a maximum\nof two marks.\n• Backup data to separate device(s) (1)\n• Use RAID_N: N=any version except RAID_0\n(1)\n• Maintenance of storage devices (1)\n• Replace devices/discs/SSDs early/before\nend of rated lifetime (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "b(i)", "marks": 3, "section": "Q2", "ctx": ["The assignment storage areas are kept on cloud storage. Data moving to and", "from the storage is kept secure by using a Virtual Private Network (VPN)."], "text": "Describe how a VPN secures moving data.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of three\nmarks.\n• Routes/forwards data to VPN server\n(instead of ISP)\n• VPN (software) encrypts data client\nside/before sending\n• VPN (software) creates VPN tunnel/uses\ntunnelling protocol\nAccept any other appropriate responses.\nDo not accept answers not related to security.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "b(ii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q2", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain one disadvantage of using a VPN in this context.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for a disadvantage and one\nmark for an appropriate linked expansion, up to\na maximum of two marks.\n• VPN requires more resources/\nprocessing/routing steps (1) which may\nslow down the internet link/data\ntransfer/system (1)\n• VPN requires configuration (1) errors may\nreveal data/reduce security/stop the\nsystem working properly (1)\n• Some countries/states/governments\nrestrict/ban VPNs (1) this may cause legal\nproblems for Redpool College/students (in\nthose places) (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "b(iii)", "marks": 3, "section": "Q2", "ctx": [], "text": "Describe how a student can check that their VPN client is working.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of three\nmarks.\n• Find public IP address using third party\nwebsite/router admin pages/command\nsuch as ipconfig (1)\n• With VPN off, check public IP address (1)\n• With VPN on, check public IP address (1)\n• Compare IP addresses, should be\ndifferent (1)\nAccept any other appropriate responses.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 2, "sub": "c", "marks": 9, "section": "Q2", "ctx": [], "text": "Evaluate the implications of using cloud storage instead of a data server for\nRedpool College’s assignment storage needs.\nYour evaluation should include the benefits and drawbacks of each, and a\nconclusion about which Redpool College should use.", "ms": {"body": "Award up to 9 marks.\nResponses should be in the context of using cloud storage instead\nof a data server.\nResponses\nwill\nbe\ncredited\naccording\nto\nthe\nstudent’s\ndemonstration of knowledge and understanding of the material,\nusing the indicative content and level descriptors below.\nThe indicative content that follows is not prescriptive. Responses\nmay cover some or all indicative content, but students should be\nrewarded for other relevant responses.\nBenefits:\nCost savings: Cloud-based networks often provide cost savings\ncompared to maintaining on-premises infrastructure.\nBenefit from economies of scale and pay only for the resources\nthey use. Assignment space needs will vary.\nScalability: Cloud networks offer on-demand scalability without\nsignificant upfront investments in hardware or software. Data\nserver would need to have enough storage for maximum needs,\nplus contingency.\nFlexibility: Cloud networks enable students to access applications\nand data from anywhere with an internet connection, promoting\nremote work and collaboration. Better for distance learning\nstudents and saves on Redpool College resources.\nAutomatic updates: Cloud service providers typically handle\nsoftware updates and security patches, ensuring the systems\nremain up-to-date and secure. Saving maintenance time at the\nCollege.\nDrawbacks:\nDependency on Internet connectivity: Cloud-based storage relies\non stable Internet connections for access and performance.\nThey are vulnerable to connectivity issues and potential\ndowntime. A data server has similar issues but Redpool College\nwill have more control/will know what is happening.\nData privacy and security concerns: Storing sensitive data in the\ncloud may raise concerns about data privacy.\nThe service provider must be trusted to maintain security and\n29\ncomply with relevant regulations.\nPotential vendor lock-in: Migrating from one cloud service\nprovider to another can be challenging/expensive.\nTechnology issues may make it difficult to switch vendors.\nOverall evaluation: The decision to use a cloud-based network\ndepends on the organisation's specific needs and requirements\nand the potential risks and benefits. Cost, scalability, and flexibility\nmust be weighed against data privacy, security, and vendor\nconcerns.\nConclusions (this is not a list of marking points – anything\nsensible and relevant should be accepted, but overall, cloud\nstorage is likely to be the best choice). Look for arguments\nsuch as:\n• Cost/scalability/flexibility weighed against data privacy/\nsecurity/vendor problems.\n• Cost/scalability/flexibility of data server weighed against\ncloud storage.\n• Accessibility by students, especially distance students.\n• Assignments probably don’t involve personal data so may\nneed less security.\n• Assignments may need more collaboration.\n30\nMark scheme (award up to 9 marks) refer to guidance for how to apply levels-\nbased mark schemes*.\nLevel\nDescriptor\nLevel 0\n0\nNo rewardable material.\nLevel 1\n1–3\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is partially relevant to the context of the\nquestion (AO2)\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis by superficially breaking\ndown the different aspects into component parts with\nerrors/inconsistency (AO3)\n• Demonstrates a basic evaluation which partially considers\ndifferent factors and competing points, leading to an\nunsupported conclusion (AO4).\nLevel 2\n4–6\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is relevant to the context of the\nquestion (AO2)\n• Demonstrates a good analysis by breaking down the\ndifferent aspects into component parts (AO3)\n• Demonstrates a good evaluation which considers different\nfactors and competing points, leading to a partially\nsupported conclusion (AO4).\nLevel 3\n7–9\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of knowledge\nand understanding that is consistently relevant to the\ncontext of the question (AO2)\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis by comprehensively\nbreaking down the different aspects into their component\nparts (AO3)\n• Demonstrates a thorough evaluation which\ncomprehensively considers different factors and competing\npoints, leading to a well-supported conclusion (AO4).", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "a(i)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q3", "ctx": ["Redpool College has its own Local Area Network (LAN). The network is run from the IT Centre in the Administration building. All internet access is routed through a high speed link in the IT Centre. Melanie uses a range of tools and techniques to help her administer the system. Penetration testing tools help Melanie identify weaknesses in the system. She", "uses a network mapping tool to create an inventory of all the devices on the", "network.", "The tool displays IP addresses and other information."], "text": "State two other pieces of information that a network mapping tool would\ndisplay.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point up to\na maximum of two marks.\n• Host/device names (1)\n• Running services (1)\n• OS details, version/patch numbers (1)\n• Ports open/used (1)\n• Network topology (map) (1)\nDo not accept answers about IP addresses.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "a(ii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q3", "ctx": [], "text": "Explain one disadvantage of using a network mapping tool for this purpose.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for identification of a\ndisadvantage and one mark for an appropriate\nlinked justification, up to a maximum of two\nmarks.\n• Tool generates a lot of traffic/noise (1)\nwhich can trigger IDS/IPS (1)\n• Tool can only detect working/running\nsystems/hosts/services (1) so giving an\nincomplete picture (of the network) (1)\n• Tool can generate false positives/incorrect\ndata (1) so giving an incorrect picture (of\nthe network) (1)\nAccept any other appropriate responses.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "b(i)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q3", "ctx": ["Melanie has installed a network intrusion detection system (IDS) and a network", "intrusion prevention system (IPS)."], "text": "Describe how an IPS differs from an IDS.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of two\nmarks.\nIPS takes action to stop (suspicious) activity (1) IDS\nonly produces a log/alert (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "b(ii)", "marks": 3, "section": "Q3", "ctx": [], "text": "Describe how an IDS works.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of three\nmarks.\n• Monitors network traffic (1)\n• Detects intrusion based on\nsignature/anomalies/heuristics/policy\nviolations/malicious activity (1)\n• Generates log (1)\n• Alerts user (1)\nAccept any other appropriate responses.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "c", "marks": 4, "section": "Q3", "ctx": [], "text": "Melanie uses a network protocol analysis tool, commonly known as a packet\nsniffer, to analyse traffic flow within the network.\nThe tool enables her to find congested routes or bottlenecks in the LAN.\nDescribe how the tool works in enabling her to find a bottleneck.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of four\nmarks.\n• Intercepts data packets (1)\n• Only intercepts small\npercentage/proportion/sample of packets\n(1)\n• Stores/logs packet header/control\ninformation (1)\n• Analyses packet header/control\ninformation (1)\n• Presents data in user-readable form/as\nchart/table/other suitable form (1)\n• Shows devices/nodes/locations with\nunusual packet loss/repeat\nrequests/failure to respond (1)\nAccept any other appropriate responses.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 3, "sub": "d", "marks": 9, "section": "Q3", "ctx": [], "text": "Melanie must produce risk assessments for the Redpool College LAN. She uses a\nrange of tools and techniques to identify vulnerabilities.\nEvaluate the importance of risk assessment and vulnerability scanning in\nmaintaining the security of the LAN.\nYour evaluation should explain the benefits of each, and include a conclusion\nabout how Redpool College could make effective use of them.", "ms": {"body": "Award up to 9 marks.\nResponses should be in the context of risk assessment and\nvulnerability scanning for Redpool College’s LAN.\nResponses\nwill\nbe\ncredited\naccording\nto\nthe\nstudent’s\ndemonstration of knowledge and understanding of the material,\nusing the indicative content and level descriptors below.\nThe indicative content that follows is not prescriptive. Responses\nmay cover some or all indicative content, but students should be\nrewarded for other relevant responses.\nIdentifying potential risks and vulnerabilities:\nVulnerability scanning helps identify possible threats and\nweaknesses in the network.\nRisk assessments are a formal way of setting\nout/categorising/prioritising the risks.\nBoth enable the college to address the issues proactively.\nBoth help identify possible risk reduction measures.\nPrioritising resources:\nHelp understand the risks and vulnerabilities, so resources can be\nfocussed on the most critical areas.\nIdentifies areas where new software/devices/technology can help\nreduce risks.\nCompliance with regulations:\nRegular risk assessments and vulnerability scanning help ensure\ncompliance with data protection laws and regulations.\nImproving ongoing/future security:\nCarrying out risk assessments and vulnerability scans helps\ncontinuously improve security measures.\nAdapting to evolving threats.\nMaintaining a robust security approach.\nHelps keep up with latest ideas/knowledge/security measures.\nReducing the impact of breaches:\nIdentifying and addressing vulnerabilities can help minimise\npotential breaches' impact, reducing costs and reputational\ndamage.\nConclusions (this is not a list of marking points – anything\nsensible and relevant should be accepted):\n• Regular updates of risk assessments.\n• Based on results of vulnerability scans.\n34\n• Based on changes in the threat landscape.\n• Ensure latest patches/updates applied to scanning tools,\nso keeping up with changes/developments in threats.\nMark scheme (award up to 9 marks) refer to guidance for how to apply levels-\nbased mark schemes*.\nLevel\nDescriptor\nLevel 0\nNo rewardable material.\nLevel 1\n1–3\n• Demonstrates basic application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is partially relevant to the context of the\nquestion (AO2)\n• Demonstrates a basic analysis by superficially breaking\ndown the different aspects into component parts with\nerrors/inconsistency (AO3)\n• Demonstrates a basic evaluation which partially considers\ndifferent factors and competing points, leading to an\nunsupported conclusion (AO4).\nLevel 2\n4-6\n• Demonstrates good application of knowledge and\nunderstanding that is relevant to the context of the\nquestion (AO2)\n• Demonstrates a good analysis by breaking down the\ndifferent aspects into component parts (AO3)\n• Demonstrates a good evaluation which considers different\nfactors and competing points, leading to a partially\nsupported conclusion (AO4).\nLevel 3\n7-9\n• Demonstrates comprehensive application of knowledge\nand understanding that is consistently relevant to the\ncontext of the question (AO2)\n• Demonstrates a thorough analysis by comprehensively\nbreaking down the different aspects into their component\nparts (AO3)\n• Demonstrates a thorough evaluation which\ncomprehensively considers different factors and competing\npoints, leading to a well-supported conclusion (AO4).", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "a(i)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q4", "ctx": ["Redpool College has its own Local Area Network (LAN) that is controlled from the IT Centre. The college employs several technical staff, including some trainees. Melanie needs to produce training materials for the trainees. This includes information about encryption methods and uses. Melanie wants to train them on Transport Layer Security (TLS)."], "text": "Describe what Transport Layer refers to in the Redpool LAN.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of two\nmarks.\n• Part of OSI/TCPIP stack/protocol/model (1)\n(layer) handles host-host/source-receiver\ndata transfer (1)\n\u0003 \u0003", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "a(ii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q4", "ctx": [], "text": "When a client and server connect using TLS they perform a handshake.\nDescribe the role of encryption in performing the handshake.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of two\nmarks.\nEITHER\n• Session keys generated (1) using a\nrandom number/ server public key (1)\nOR\n• Session keys generated (1) using (Diffie–\nHellman) key exchange (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "a(iii)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q4", "ctx": [], "text": "Describe the relationship between TLS and HTTPS.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each of the following points,\nup to a maximum of two marks.\n• HTTPS piggybacks HTTP over TLS (1)\n• This creates a secure channel (1)\n• This allows the encryption of the HTTP\ntraffic (1)\nAccept any other appropriate responses.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "b(i)", "marks": 2, "section": "Q4", "ctx": ["Melanie wants to train them on digital signatures and digital certificates."], "text": "Describe the difference between a digital signature and a digital certificate.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of two\nmarks.\n• Digital signature verifies data is\nsecure/can be trusted/is as sent (1) digital\ncertificate verifies sender is who they say\nthey are/can be trusted (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "b(ii)", "marks": 3, "section": "Q4", "ctx": [], "text": "Describe the role of encryption in creating a digital signature.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point in a\nlinked description up to a maximum of three\nmarks.\n• Hash created of message/data (1)\n• Hash is one-way (1)\n• Hash (value) encrypted with (private) key\n(1)\n• Encrypted hash is the digital signature (1)\nDo not accept answers that are about processes\nother than encryption, e.g. sending/receiving\nmessages. Do not accept answers about\nprocesses after signature creation, e.g.\ndecrypting signature.", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "c(i)", "marks": 3, "section": "Q4", "ctx": ["Melanie knows that modern cryptographic cyphers are often derived from older,", "pre-computer methods."], "text": "The one-time-pad is a pre-computer cryptographic method that is impossible\nto break if used correctly. It is now important in the realm of quantum\ncomputing.\nGive three requirements for using a one-time-pad.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point up to\na maximum of three marks.\n• Key/pad must not be reused/must only be\nused once (1)\n• Key/pad must be kept secret (1)\n• Key/pad must be larger/at least as large\nas the plaintext/message (1)\n• Key/pad must be random (1)", "levels": null}}, {"q": 4, "sub": "c(ii)", "marks": 9, "section": "Q4", "ctx": [], "text": "The substitution cypher has been developed into modern block cyphers.\nThe Caesar Cypher is an example of a pre-computer substitution cypher. It\napplies a key (shift value) to a plaintext message and returns an encrypted\ntext. The message consists of single case letters, with no spaces, numbers or\nother characters.\nDevelop a simple algorithm for a Caesar Cypher encryption method.\nThe algorithm should be given in the form of a set of statements in English.\nDo not submit anything written in a scripting or programming language.", "ms": {"body": "Award one mark for each appropriate point up to\na maximum of nine marks.\n1. Input message (1)\n2. Input key/value shift (1)\n3. Find/identify first letter (1)\n4. Identify letter number/find position in\nalphabet (1)\n5. Add/count forward key/value shift (1)\n6. If count goes past Z, resume at A (1)\n7. Record cyphertext letter (1)\n8. Remove/move one on from current letter\n(1)\n9. If no more letters in message then stop (1)\n10. Otherwise go back to instruction 4 (1) (this\ncould be an arrow/line)\nDo not accept statements that are obviously in a\nscripting or programming language.\n38\nJuly 2024\nFor information about Pearson Qualifications, including Pearson Edexcel\nand BTEC qualifications visit qualifications.pearson.com\nEdexcel and BTEC are registered trademarks of Pearson Education Limited\nPearson Education Limited. Registered in England and Wales No. 872828\nRegistered Office: 80 Strand, London WC2R 0RL.\nVAT Reg No GB 278 537121\nBTEC", "levels": null}}], "badge": "Pearson · current spec", "badgeTone": "new", "notice": "Pearson’s sample assessment for the current AAQ specification: the closest match to the real exam. Figures, tables and information booklets are described in words."}]);
